+## Bottom line
+
+County commissioners cannot set Child Care and Development Fund (CCDF) eligibility or provider-payment rules, but they can reduce the local access gap between qualifying and actually receiving assistance. The strongest county lever is a navigation-first partnership that uses existing family touchpoints to find likely eligible households, help them complete state applications, and connect them with participating providers. Commissioners should judge success by completed referrals and placements, while treating national estimates as context rather than a county forecast.
+
+## Evidence
+
+- **Reach.** A [January 2025 national access report](https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-25-107754) estimated that, in an average month in FY 2021, 1.8 million children received subsidies while 11.5 million met federal eligibility rules—about 15% participation. Federal eligibility sets an outer boundary; state eligibility policies may use lower income limits and priorities, narrowing who can qualify locally. The FY 2021 estimates were preliminary and reflect pandemic-era work patterns, so this data lag makes them a baseline, not a current county caseload estimate.
+- **Affordability.** The [2024 final rule](https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2024/03/01/2024-04139/improving-child-care-access-affordability-and-stability-in-the-child-care-and-development-fund) bars family co-payments above 7% of family income and permits broader waivers for some families. Yet CCDF is not an entitlement: meeting federal or state eligibility conditions does not ensure receipt. Outreach therefore needs accurate screening language—“potentially eligible,” not “guaranteed”—and a handoff that helps families navigate documentation, provider availability, and any waiting list without overstating county authority.
+- **Provider stability.** The rule generally requires payment before or at the beginning of service and payment based on authorized enrollment rather than attendance, with limited alternatives. Those changes address fixed operating costs and may encourage provider participation. However, every state and the District of Columbia had requested temporary waivers for at least some provisions by the [January 2025 review](https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-25-107754). Counties should verify current state practice before advising providers and use local feedback to distinguish an enrollment bottleneck from a shortage of participating slots.
+
+## Policy options
+
+1. **Navigation-first county partnership.** Add CCDF screening and warm referrals to existing county-facing services used by families with young children. Use the [program's statutory consumer-education and referral framework](https://www.congress.gov/113/plaws/publ186/PLAW-113publ186.htm) to coordinate with established resource-and-referral organizations rather than inventing a parallel eligibility system. Track anonymized referral completion, application friction, and whether referred families locate a participating provider. This option directly targets the difference between possible eligibility and successful use while respecting state decision-making.
+2. **Provider-access feedback compact.** Convene participating and nonparticipating providers, family navigators, and employers to document recurring barriers such as payment timing, attendance rules, application handoffs, and unavailable care types. Publish an aggregate county access snapshot and submit the findings through existing planning channels. The [December 2024 program overview](https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/R47312) confirms substantial state flexibility within federal boundaries, so locally grounded evidence can sharpen requests without implying that commissioners control eligibility or reimbursement.
+
+## Recommendation
+
+Choose Option 1. It responds most directly to the documented participation gap, fits county authority, and can produce useful local evidence without promising benefits or rewriting state rules. Build provider feedback into navigation case review as supporting evidence, but keep the commission's primary decision centered on whether existing county touchpoints can deliver clearer screening, completed applications, and successful provider connections.
+
